# A worklist drained from its top without copying it (defect 498,
# 2026-10-07; spec § 10, a slice is a NEW array; design.md §4.10).
#
# THE SHAPE IT REPLACES. `work @ work.slice(from: 0, to: work.len() - 1)`
# pops by building a new array of every element but the last, so a drain of
# n entries copied about n²/2 of them; and the list was then still shared at
# its next push, which copied it whole a second time: 1,000 pushes copied
# 999,000 elements (lane b14-p409's probe), and `builtins.reachable` alone
# copied 2,091,585,474 on the compiler's own `--emit-c`, 1,899,160,831 at
# its pops and 192,424,643 at its pushes.
#
# HOW. The live part of the list is its first `size` elements, and the
# caller holds `size`: `take` lowers it and reads the element there, `put`
# writes the slot past it, or appends where every slot is live, and raises
# it. A bare `@` array written in place is its caller's own block (panel
# 196's R1), so neither copies anything. The elements visited, and their
# order, are the slicing drain's exactly, which is what keeps every
# worklist's output, and the C it decides, what it was: the test below holds
# the two drains to one sequence.

# Put `value` at the top of a list whose live part is its first `size`
# elements.
function put(@items: [i64], @size: i64, value: i64)
    if size < items.len()
        items[size] @ value
    else
        items @ items.push(value)
    size @ size + 1

# The element at the top, which leaves the live part.
function take(items: [i64], @size: i64) -> i64
    size @ size - 1
    return items[size]

## Tests

# The oracle is the slicing drain this module replaced, run beside it over
# one sequence of pushes and pops: a push of each value's two successors
# below a bound, so the list grows and shrinks and reuses its slots.
test "a drain visits what the slicing drain visited, in its order (defect 498)"
    sliced: [i64] @= [1, 2]
    held: [i64] @= []
    size: i64 @= 0

    for one in [1, 2]
        put(@held, size: @size, value: one)

    old_order: [i64] @= []
    new_order: [i64] @= []

    while sliced.len() > 0
        top = sliced[sliced.len() - 1]
        sliced @ sliced.slice(from: 0, to: sliced.len() - 1)
        old_order @ old_order.push(top)

        if top < 40
            sliced @ sliced.push(top * 2)
            sliced @ sliced.push(top * 2 + 1)

    while size > 0
        top = take(held, size: @size)
        new_order @ new_order.push(top)

        if top < 40
            put(@held, size: @size, value: top * 2)
            put(@held, size: @size, value: top * 2 + 1)

    assert old_order.len() > 40
    assert new_order == old_order
    assert held.len() < old_order.len()
